{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.Ribbon","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.Ribbon","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Monoidal.Braided.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Monoidal.Rigid.Basic"],"declarations":22,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RibbonCategory.rightAdjointMate_inv_twist","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RibbonCategory.rightAdjointMate_inv_twist","kind":"theorem","line":201,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L201-L208","doc":"**The mate of the inverse twist is the inverse of the dual's twist**: `(θ_X⁻¹)ᘁ = (θ_{Xᘁ})⁻¹`.\nDuality is contravariant, so this does not follow from `twist_dual` by rewriting — it needs the two\ncomposites, via `comp_rightAdjointMate`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RibbonCategory.twist_dualDual","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RibbonCategory.twist_dualDual","kind":"theorem","line":193,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L193-L199","doc":"**The twist commutes with double-dualisation**: `θ_{Xᘁᘁ} = (θ_X)ᘁᘁ`.  The ribbon axiom applied\ntwice.  The double dual is where the general quantum-dimension conjugation symmetry\n`dim (Xᘁ) = dim X` would live; that broader statement additionally needs a pivotal structure, which\nMathlib does not provide either. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RibbonCategory.ofSymmetric","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RibbonCategory.ofSymmetric","kind":"instance","line":181,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L181-L188","doc":"**Non-vacuity: every symmetric right-rigid category is ribbon with the trivial twist `θ = 𝟙`.**\nThe ribbon axiom reduces to Mathlib's `rightAdjointMate_id`.  The classical limit therefore stays\n*inside* the class — `θ = 𝟙` is a describable member, not an exclusion. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RibbonCategory.twist_dual","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RibbonCategory.twist_dual","kind":"theorem","line":175,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L175-L175","doc":"**Ribbon (dual-compatibility) axiom**: `θ_{Xᘁ} = (θ_X)ᘁ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RibbonCategory.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RibbonCategory.mk","kind":"ctor","line":172,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L172-L172","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RibbonCategory.toBalancedCategory","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RibbonCategory.toBalancedCategory","kind":"def","line":172,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L172-L175","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RibbonCategory","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RibbonCategory","kind":"class","line":168,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L168-L175","doc":"**A ribbon category**: a balanced right-rigid category whose twist is compatible with duality,\n`θ_{Xᘁ} = (θ_X)ᘁ`, where `(·)ᘁ` is Mathlib's `rightAdjointMate`.  This is the datum the\nReshetikhin–Turaev construction needs on top of braided-rigid: `twist_dual` is what makes the ribbon\ntrace well-behaved under duality. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.ofSymmetric","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.ofSymmetric","kind":"instance","line":152,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L152-L164","doc":"**Non-vacuity: every symmetric monoidal category is balanced with the trivial twist `θ = 𝟙`.**\nThe double braiding is the identity (`SymmetricCategory.symmetry`), so the balancing axiom\n`𝟙 = β_{X,Y} ≫ β_{Y,X} ≫ (𝟙 ⊗ 𝟙)` reduces to it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twist_tensorUnit_right","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twist_tensorUnit_right","kind":"theorem","line":142,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L142-L148","doc":"**The balancing axiom is consistent with `θ_𝟙 = 𝟙`.**  Specialising `twist_tensor` at\n`Y = 𝟙_C` and transporting along the right unitor recovers exactly naturality, so the unit case\nimposes no constraint beyond the two axioms.  Derived — this is a check on the axiom set, not a new\nassumption. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twist_tensor_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twist_tensor_three","kind":"theorem","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L110-L140","doc":"**The twist on a three-fold tensor carries no irreducible three-body datum.**\n\nUnfolding the balancing axiom twice: `θ_{X ⊗ Y ⊗ Z}` is built entirely from **pairwise** braidings\n(`β_{X, Y⊗Z}`, `β_{Y⊗Z, X}`, `β_{Y,Z}`, `β_{Z,Y}`) and **single-object** twists\n(`θ_X`, `θ_Y`, `θ_Z`).  Nothing in it depends on the three objects jointly in a way that is not\nalready a composite of two-object data.  Three is the first arity at which a genuine many-body term\n*could* appear, and it does not.\n\n## Scope of the resulting prune (read before citing this downstream)\n\nThis is a **structural** fact about the ribbon datum, in the sense of the \"(b) structural exclusion\"\ntier: the invariant is the balancing axiom itself, stated above and cited here.  What it excludes is\nprecise and narrow:\n\n* it **does** exclude any weight *defined as a composite of twists and braidings* on an `A`-fold\n  tensor from carrying an irreducible three-or-more-body contribution — such a composite is always\n  per-object data together with pairwise data;\n* it **does not** exclude the missing super-pairwise additive term in the many-body binding\n  magnitude.  That term is not claimed here to be ribbon-derived, and if it is not, this says\n  nothing about it.\n\nThe bridge between the two is a question about *how the binding weight is defined*, not a question\nabout ribbon categories, and it is deliberately left open here.  What the lemma does supply, if that\nbridge is made, is a reason of the right kind: the pairwise cap `ρ_A ≤ C(A,2)` recorded in the\nmass-theory development has exactly the shape this decomposition predicts — `C(A,2)` is the number\nof pairwise braidings the iterated balancing axiom produces. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.inv_twist_naturality","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.inv_twist_naturality","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L105-L108","doc":"**The inverse twist is natural too**: `θ_X⁻¹ ≫ f = f ≫ θ_Y⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twistNatIso_hom_app","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twistNatIso_hom_app","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L103-L103","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twistNatIso","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twistNatIso","kind":"def","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L97-L101","doc":"The twist packaged as a natural automorphism of the identity functor.  This is what the\nnaturality axiom asserts; having it in this form is what lets the twist be composed with other\nnatural transformations. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twistIso_hom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twistIso_hom","kind":"theorem","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L95-L95","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twistIso","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twistIso","kind":"def","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L92-L93","doc":"The twist as an isomorphism `X ≅ X`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twist_tensor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twist_tensor","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L78-L78","doc":"**Balancing axiom**: `θ_{X ⊗ Y} = β_{X,Y} ≫ β_{Y,X} ≫ (θ_X ⊗ θ_Y)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twist_naturality","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twist_naturality","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L76-L76","doc":"Naturality: `θ_X ≫ f = f ≫ θ_Y`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twist_unit","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twist_unit","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L74-L74","doc":"`θ_{𝟙} = 𝟙`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twist_isIso","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twist_isIso","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L72-L72","doc":"The twist is an isomorphism. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twist","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.twist","kind":"def","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L70-L70","doc":"The balancing twist `θ_X : X ⟶ X`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory.mk","kind":"ctor","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L68-L68","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.BalancedCategory","kind":"class","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/Ribbon.lean#L65-L79","doc":"**A balanced (braided) category**: a braided monoidal category equipped with a natural twist\n`θ_X : X ⟶ X` satisfying `θ_{𝟙} = 𝟙` and the Joyal–Street balancing axiom\n`θ_{X ⊗ Y} = β_{X,Y} ≫ β_{Y,X} ≫ (θ_X ⊗ θ_Y)`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}